\section{Discussion and Conclusion}\label{sec:discussion}\label{sec:selection_method}\label{sec:conclusion}

\paragraph{Why test-time scaling fails for NER.}
For discrete-answer reasoning, sample diversity reflects diverse reasoning paths; for NER, degeneracy ($12$--$68$\%) eliminates candidate diversity while signal alignment collapse ($\alpha{\to}0$) prevents ranking.
We quantify how the well-known capability-diversity tradeoff \cite{yun2025price} manifests specifically in NER test-time scaling, observing this pattern across model families (5.5--74\%; Table~\ref{tab:frontier_sc_cross}) and scales (4B--72B; \S\ref{sec:scaling}).
Free-form decoding confirms this is model-intrinsic rather than decoder-induced (within-instance LP std ratio $0.97{\times}$; \S\ref{sec:selection_gap}).

\paragraph{Practical implications.}
On our benchmarks, measuring degeneracy and within-instance LP-F1 alignment at $N{=}8$ sufficed to predict whether scaling would yield gains. We suggest a low-cost diagnostic: draw $N{=}8$ samples and compute instance degeneracy; if ${>}50$\%, repeated sampling is unlikely to help. If ${<}20$\%, compute within-instance LP-F1 correlation to assess whether LP selection or entity construction is viable. $\theta_{\text{crit}}{=}0.70$ was stable across our four NER datasets but may require task-specific calibration.

\paragraph{Open questions.}
Whether the capability-diversity tension persists for other structured tasks (code generation, summarization, semantic parsing) remains open. The degeneracy-alignment decomposition may generalize, but the specific thresholds and signal behaviors likely differ: SQL exhibits 87\% degeneracy from syntactic constraints rather than model convergence (\S\ref{sec:discussion}), suggesting task-specific bottlenecks require independent characterization.

\paragraph{Conclusion.}
We characterize when test-time scaling fails for NER, with exploratory RE and SQL extensions.
The diagnostic framework---localizing failure to degeneracy and signal alignment collapse---is our primary contribution; whether the capability-diversity tension extends beyond NER remains open.

